---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { FAQ } from "../lib/faq";
import { faqPage, organization, softwareApplication, website } from "../lib/jsonld";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";
import { loadSummary } from "../lib/survey";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const summary = await loadSummary();
const posts = (await getCollection("research", (p) => !p.data.draft && p.data.survey)).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.getTime() - a.data.date.getTime(),
);
const surveyPost = posts[0];
const postBase = surveyPost === undefined ? "/research/" : `/research/${surveyPost.id}/`;
const exampleApis = ["discourse-meta", "github"]
  .map((id) => summary.apis.find((a) => a.id === id))
  .filter((a) => a !== undefined);
const jsonld = [organization(), website(), softwareApplication(), faqPage(FAQ)];
---

<Base title={SITE.homeTitle} description={SITE.description} bareTitle jsonld={jsonld}>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>{SITE.tagline}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An open-source CLI and MCP server that runs your OpenAPI spec against a live
      implementation and tells you which side to fix.
    </p>

    <div class="cmds">
      <div class="cmd">
        <pre><code>npx apidrifter plan openapi.yaml</code></pre>
        <span class="cap">Turn the spec into a request plan with the slots an agent or you fill in.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cmd">
        <pre><code>npx apidrifter run openapi.yaml --base-url http://localhost:3000</code></pre>
        <span class="cap">Execute the plan against a running server and verify every response.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cmd">
        <pre><code>npx apidrifter serve</code></pre>
        <span class="cap">The same loop as an MCP server, so any agent can drive it.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="n">01</div>
        <h3>Plan</h3>
        <p>
          The engine reads the spec and proposes one request per operation, with the parameters,
          body and auth it needs. Values come from the spec&rsquo;s own examples; an agent or a
          small file supplies the rest.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="n">02</div>
        <h3>Run</h3>
        <p>
          Requests go to your dev, staging or production server. Responses are matched to the
          operation and validated: status, media type, headers and body, with the schema&rsquo;s
          direction taken into account.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="n">03</div>
        <h3>Report</h3>
        <p>
          Every finding carries two fixes: what to change in the implementation if the spec is
          the truth, and what to change in the spec if the implementation is. Humans read it;
          agents act on it. The <a href="/docs/rules/">rules reference</a> lists every finding
          the engine can report.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="what-is-api-drift">What is API drift?</h2>
    <p>
      API drift is the gap between what an API&rsquo;s published OpenAPI document says and what
      the live implementation actually does: a field typed as an integer that arrives as a
      string, a required property that is sometimes missing, a null the schema forbids, a
      status code nobody documented. It breaks generated clients, typed SDKs, mocks and
      contract tests, because all of them trust the document. It appears whenever a spec is
      written by hand and maintained separately from the code, or generated once and edited
      afterwards.
    </p>
    <ul class="forms">
      <li>
        <b>Spec ahead of implementation.</b> The document describes fields or responses the
        server does not produce yet, or never will.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Implementation ahead of spec.</b> The server changed and the document was not
        updated, which is the most common form in our <a href={postBase}>survey of public APIs</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Both drifting from an older version.</b> Spec and server each moved in different
        directions from a shared ancestor, so neither is a reliable description of the other.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="spec-differ">Also a spec differ</h2>
    <p>
      The same engine compares two versions of a spec and classifies every change as breaking,
      warning or info, knowing that a field becoming required is breaking in a request and
      harmless in a response. The <a href="/docs/design/">design document</a> explains this
      polarity model.
    </p>
    <pre><code>npx apidrifter breaking old.yaml new.yaml   # exit 1 on breaking changes, for CI
npx apidrifter mcp snapshot https://mcp.example.com/mcp --out before.json
npx apidrifter mcp diff before.json https://mcp.example.com/mcp</code></pre>
    <p>
      MCP servers are covered too: tools, resources and prompts are snapshotted and diffed, and
      a changed tool description is tracked as a change, because descriptions are what agents
      act on.
    </p>
  </section>

  {
    surveyPost !== undefined && (
      <section class="teaser">
        <p class="big">
          {summary.totals.withHard} of {summary.totals.apis} public APIs do not match their own
          spec.
        </p>
        <p>
          We sent read-only requests to {summary.totals.apis} public APIs that publish an OpenAPI
          document and found {summary.totals.hard} hard violations on successful responses:
          wrong types, missing required fields, nulls the schema forbids. Every request is
          reproducible.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={postBase}>Read the survey</a>
          {exampleApis.length > 0 && (
            <>
              {" "}&middot; examples:{" "}
              {exampleApis.map((a, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && ", "}
                  <a href={`${postBase}${a.id}/`}>{a.name}</a>
                </>
              ))}
            </>
          )}
        </p>
      </section>
    )
  }

  <section>
    <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      {
        FAQ.map((item) => (
          <>
            <dt>{item.q}</dt>
            <dd>{item.a}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
    <pre><code>npm install -g apidrifter    # or run it with npx, as above</code></pre>
    <p class="muted small">
      Node 22.12 or newer. Apache-2.0. The CLI sends requests only where you point it; URLs
      other than localhost must be HTTPS. Secrets passed as headers are never written to
      output.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/docs/cli/">CLI reference</a> &middot; <a href="/docs/rules/">Rules reference</a>
      &middot; <a href="/docs/design/">Design</a> &middot; <a href="/agents/">For coding agents</a>
      &middot; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &middot; <a href="/compare/">Compare</a> &middot; <a href="/drift-index/">Drift index</a> &middot;
      <a href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener">GitHub</a> &middot;
      <a href={SITE.npm} rel="noopener">npm</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
